• For each, explain which audience it appeals to most

About me: [DESCRIBE YOURSELF, ROLE, EXPERTISE]
Target audience: [WHO YOU WANT TO ATTRACT]
Tone: [e.g., “confident but not arrogant, slightly witty”]

PART 2: BUSINESS & STRATEGY (Prompts 11–20)
11. Competitive Intelligence Analyst


You are a senior market analyst who specialises in crowded markets.

Conduct a competitive analysis for [YOUR PRODUCT / COMPANY] against: [LIST COMPETITORS].

For each competitor, give:
1. Core value proposition (1 sentence)
2. Target audience
3. Pricing model
4. Top 3 strengths
5. Top 3 weaknesses
6. What they do better than us
7. What we do better than them

Then:
• Create a simple positioning matrix (two axes that matter most in this market)
• Identify the biggest gap no one is filling
• Give 3 strategic recommendations for how we should differentiate

Be specific. Use real-world logic, not generic “be more innovative” jargon.
12. Business Model Validator


You are a startup advisor who has evaluated hundreds of business models.

Evaluate this business idea:
• Idea: [DESCRIBE]
• Target market: [WHO]
• Revenue model: [HOW IT MAKES MONEY]
• Current stage: [IDEA / MVP / LAUNCHED]

Give:
1. A score from 1–10 on: market size, defensibility, monetization clarity, execution complexity, and timing
2. The single biggest risk to this business
3. The single biggest opportunity most founders miss
4. 3 things I should validate before investing more time
5. A “red team” analysis: argue why this will fail
6. A “blue team” response: argue why it can still succeed

Be direct. No sugar-coating.
13. Pricing Strategy Builder


You are a pricing strategist who designs psychologically smart pricing.

Help me build a pricing structure for [PRODUCT / SERVICE].

Details:
• What it does: [DESCRIPTION]
• Target customer: [WHO]
• Current pricing (if any): [PRICE]
• Competitor pricing: [COMPETITOR PRICES]
• Cost to deliver: [COST PER USER / UNIT]

Give:
1. 3 pricing tier options:
